WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:03.580
Why do people keep making movies about a guy
who shoots webs out of his wrists?

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:05.920
Because they're fun, and that's not just true
for the audience.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:08.260
The people making them seem to be having a
blast, too.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:11.400
Here are some times when things got a little
silly on set.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:15.920
In Spider-Man: Homecoming, Tom Holland's Peter
Parker has a full and busy life.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:20.180
In addition to superheroics, he's got to juggle
homework with a spot on his school's academic

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:23.100
team, not to mention working as an intern
at Stark Industries.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:27.210
In one scene, he tries to talk his way out
of an upcoming academic decathlon, and his

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.180
advisor won't take no for an answer.

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:33.720
Peter sputters off a list of his responsibilities,
and it turns out that "responsibilities" is

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:34.800
something of a trigger word.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.140
It's the crux of a Spider-Man mantra, after
all.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:41.040
When he flubs the line, he can't help but
break character, give a wry turn to the camera,

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:44.080
and recite the phrase predecessor Tobey Maguire
made mainstream:

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:50.080
"Mr. Stark and my internship, you know, I...I've
got a lot of responsibility with it.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:53.400
With great power comes great responsibility."

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:57.560
When Spider-Man 2 opens, Peter Parker has
already endured a lot as Spider-Man.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:01.400
He's in college at that point, but still the
same clumsy guy in public, picking up none

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:03.260
of the gracefulness of his superhero side.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:07.460
In one scene, Peter drops all of his books
and school supplies while walking on campus.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:08.780
That was supposed to happen.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:11.640
What came as a surprise to Tobey Maguire was
what happened next.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:15.720
As he stays in character, grumbling and picking
up his things as other students walk by, one

00:01:15.721 --> 00:01:20.000
faceless extra slams their backpack into Maguire's
head and catches a strap on his neck.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.280
This blooper is actually a prank, courtesy
of director Sam Raimi.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.760
He's the guy with the backpack, surprising
Maguire and making him laugh.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:37.160
While Spider-Man isn't as loaded as Batman
or powerful as Superman, he does have one

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.200
big thing in common with them: He's an orphan.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.020
Those other guys were raised by a butler and
some kindly farmers, while Spider-Man grew

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:46.060
into a goofy and friendly teen thanks to his
Aunt May and Uncle Ben.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:49.850
In a rapid-fire barrage of bloopers from the
set of Spider-Man: Homecoming, Ned, played

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:53.940
by Jacob Batalon, and Peter try to talk to
Liz about the current teen party scene, and

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:57.140
Peter mentions how parents sometimes let their
kids throw those parties.

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:59.360
That bit of dialogue sends Holland off the
rails.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.980
He remembers that his character doesn't exactly
have biological parents anymore.

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:05.400
"They let you have parties, you know?

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.740
My parents are…dead."

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:13.560
A quiet, heartfelt moment with the object
of your affection can make even the most confident

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:15.160
person into a tongue-tied mess.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:18.320
But hey, sometimes deep thoughts and feelings
are better left unsaid, right?

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:23.020
That would make one Spider-Man 2 blooper extremely
realistic, except Tobey Maguire isn't supposed

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:27.570
to be so wordlessly flummoxed in a cute, romantic
scene with Kirsten Dunst, who's playing Mary

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:28.360
Jane Watson.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:29.940
"I…"

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:39.480
To his credit, he sells that mistake as simply
being overwhelmed by emotion.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:43.240
But then he realizes he's been silent for
way too long, and that the jig is up.

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:47.520
Part of what makes Spider-Man such a great
character are the differences between the

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:50.020
bold superhero and his mild-mannered alter-ego.

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:54.160
Spider-Man is Peter Parker, of course, but
they're also total opposites, at least to

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:55.160
the casual observer.

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:58.180
For example, Spider-Man glides through the
air with the greatest of ease.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:01.860
Peter Parker, on the other hand, is a gangly
and timid teen who's not quite comfortable

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:02.820
in his own skin.

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:06.599
In Spider-Man: Homecoming, the actor meant
to embody both Peter and Spider-Man really

00:03:06.599 --> 00:03:08.320
nailed Peter Parker's awkwardness.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:12.270
Tom Holland is almost too deep in character;
the actor apparently tripped, slipped, and

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:14.760
stumbled quite a lot during the making of
the movie.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:19.340
Maybe Tobey Maguire feels uncomfortable doing
romantic scenes in action-heavy superhero

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:20.000
movies.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.460
Or maybe he doesn't think the idea of introducing
the comics character Gwen Stacy is necessary

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:28.460
for the already overstuffed Spider-Man 3,
which already has so many characters.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:32.140
Or maybe the actor has just gone a little
stir-crazy from playing Spider-Man in three

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:33.600
movies over five years.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:37.300
Whatever the reason, Maguire can't seem to
get through filming close-up coverage on what's

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:41.310
supposed to be a simple exchange in which
Gwen introduces herself to the Daily Bugle

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:42.040
photographer.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:43.160
"We got it under control."

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:46.980
"And action."

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:48.140
"Hi, Peter!"

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:49.140
"Hi!"

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:52.520
Was it the blonde wig?

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:57.080
Spider-Man's web swinging is a special effect
achieved with some combination of green-screen,

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:00.860
CGI, and actors suspended from gravity-defying
wires.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:04.240
Being harnessed up like that is not exactly
a natural, or comfortable state.

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:08.400
It takes a lot of effort to get used to, let
alone do a good job of acting in that condition.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:11.700
On the set of Spider-Man: Homecoming, Tom
Holland couldn't seem to get the hang of those

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:12.760
dangling wires.

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:16.240
He tries to turn his inability to navigate
the wires into a little dance in front of

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:18.140
his concerned co-star Donald Glover.

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:21.000
"Whoa!

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:22.300
Hey, are you all right, man?"

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:22.880
"I got it!"

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:27.660
In more wire-based shenanigans, while shooting
a night scene for Spider-Man 3, Tobey Maguire

00:04:27.669 --> 00:04:31.680
guides Kirsten Dunst down to the safety of
the ground, while notably not wearing his

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:32.600
Spidey suit.

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:36.300
Dunst has so much fun with the descent that
she lets out a couple of hoots, which seemingly

00:04:36.309 --> 00:04:38.020
causes Maguire to forget his line.

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:40.000
He improvises some silliness instead:

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:41.880
"Whoo!"

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:45.840
"Let's go get on my bike!"

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:46.660
"Okay!"

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:52.980
In Avengers: Infinity War, when Thanos snaps
half the universe to death, Spider-Man falls

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:55.999
into the arms of his mentor Tony Stark before
turning to dust.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:57.460
"I don't wanna go.

00:04:57.460 --> 00:04:58.640
I don't wanna go, sir.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:04:59.300
Please.

00:04:59.300 --> 00:04:59.840
Please.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:00.720
I don't wanna go.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:02.020
I don't wanna go."

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:06.100
It's heartbreaking, which means Holland probably
wanted to keep things light and bouncy when

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:08.760
his character miraculously returns in Avengers:
Endgame.

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:12.620
In fact, the actor literally bounces around
the rubble-strewn set, much to the pleasure

00:05:12.629 --> 00:05:16.400
of Downey Jr., who hugs the boy wonder for
just a little too long, and even plants a

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:17.400
kiss on his cheek.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:18.880
Welcome back, Wall Crawler.

